# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://snl.no/> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (the Store norske leksikon API, live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "Candidates: from its own general knowledge, the authoring agent drew up, in five batches, about 250 search strings for widely taught events of world history (some events were searched more than once), deliberately including Africa, the Americas, Asia and Oceania as well as Europe and covering antiquity to the 21st century. No published list or timeline was copied. Each candidate was looked up with the Wikidata wbsearchentities API (English search, four hits; the exact requests are under Queries) and the right item picked by hand from the hits and their descriptions. Four titles that the search did not find were resolved to item ids through the English Wikipedia pageprops API (only the wikibase_item ids were used)."@en ,
                 "Dates and labels: two SPARQL queries against https://query.wikidata.org/sparql (under Queries) returned, for every candidate item, its English and Swedish labels, Swedish aliases (first query), English and Swedish Wikipedia sitelinks, and the values of P585 point in time, P580 start time, P582 end time, P571 inception, P577 publication date, P619 launch date, P1619 date of official opening and P576 dissolution date. The raw JSON responses were saved and read item by item. One further query fetched the inception (P571) of Australia (Q408)."@en ,
                 "Choosing the year: for each card the property that dates exactly the event on the front was chosen (for instance P582 end time of the siege of Constantinople for its fall, P580 start time of the Korean War for its outbreak, P1619 official opening of the Suez Canal, P619 launch of Vostok 1 for the first human in space). The card's evidence names the property and its value. Where an item had several conflicting dates, the one matching the card was used and the discrepancy recorded in quality control."@en ,
                 "Years before Christ: the Wikidata Query Service writes dates in the XSD 1.1 form, in which 1 BCE is year 0 (https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Wikibase/Indexing/RDF_Dump_Format: “The xsd:dateTime dates follow XSD 1.1 standard, which uses the proleptic Gregorian calendar, and represents the year 1 BCE as +0000.”). A Query Service year of -489 is therefore 490 BC. The cards give the traditional year (490 BC); their machine checks use the Query Service form (-489). The same page explains why some dates appear shifted by a few days (Julian dates converted to the proleptic Gregorian calendar, e.g. 25 December 800 shown as 800-12-29); for no card does this change the year."@en ,
                 "Wording: every front is the deck's own short wording of the event, in English and in Swedish. Where a front uses an item's Wikidata label verbatim (English or Swedish), a label check is attached so the build confirms it. Swedish text not taken from an event's label was written by the authoring agent in Swedish conventions (sentence case; lower-case names of peoples and adjectives). In the first draft the Swedish names of people and places inside such text were written from the agent's knowledge; the Swedish labels or aliases of related items were checked only where a name seemed uncertain (Q408 alias “Australiska statsförbundet”, Q33296 “Mogulriket”). In quality-control round 2 every such name was checked against Wikidata's sv labels and aliases and, where needed, Swedish Wikipedia (queries under Queries): Odovakar (sv label of Q103333; the draft's “Odoaker” was corrected), Qin Shi Huangdi (sv label of Q7192; corrected from “Qin Shi Huang”), Alarik (Q102371 sv label “Alarik I”), Johan utan land (sv alias of Q129308), Karl den store (Q3044), Konstantinopel (Q16869), Mecka (title of the Swedish Wikipedia article on Q5806, whose sv label is the variant “Mekka”), Jurij Gagarin (Q7327), Dagen D (redirect on Swedish Wikipedia to “Landstigningen i Normandie”, whose lead names 6 June 1944 “Dagen D”). Short frontNotes disambiguate battles that share a name with other battles; backNotes explain conventions (the Julian date of the October Revolution, AD years below 100, the Hijra as year 1 of the Islamic calendar)."@en ,
                 "Back convention: years AD are untagged numbers (“1789”); years before Christ are tagged text, “490 BC” in English and “490 f.Kr.” in Swedish. AD 9 and AD 79 carry a backNote saying AD so they are not mistaken for an unmarked BC year."@en ,
                 "Independent check: every card's year was checked against Store norske leksikon, the Norwegian national encyclopedia, whose articles are written and signed by subject experts. For each card the SNL search API (https://snl.no/api/v1/search) was queried, the article JSON of the top hits fetched (https://snl.no/<article>.json) and its sentences containing the year read; where the first search found nothing, further searches were run (all listed under Queries). The verbatim sentence, with an English gloss, is in each card's evidence. Before SNL, a few Encyclopaedia Britannica, encyclopedia.com and infoplease article URLs were requested as a possible independent check; the exact URLs were not recorded. Britannica refused the automated requests (HTTP 403) and the other two returned not-found, so none of them returned content and none was used. The Battle of Talas (751) was dropped because no SNL article stating its year was found."@en ,
                 "Build and validation: the dossier was built with the deck builder, which re-ran every Wikidata check (the date of every card, plus the labels used) against live Wikidata, and the deck was validated with node packages/deck-library/scripts/validate_sources.ts world-history-dates (the app's SHACL shapes and DCAT-AP)."@en ,
                 "Notes and quality control: after the reviews, every frontNote and backNote was compared with the card's evidence, and where a note stated a fact its evidence did not contain, either a supporting SNL sentence or Wikidata statement was added to the evidence or the note was cut back to what the evidence says (logged in quality-control round 3). The SNL articles were re-fetched as https://snl.no/<article>.json and searched for sentences supporting each note; the Wikidata statements were fetched with the queries listed under Queries."@en ,
                 "Selection: Included: landmark events that are widely taught in world history, that happened in a single year that the sources agree on, and that can be named unambiguously in a short phrase. Processes and wars are only included through a single dated moment (a war breaking out, a city falling, a treaty being signed, a revolution beginning), and the front says which moment is meant. The set is spread across eras (antiquity to 2001) and continents (Africa, the Americas, Asia, Europe, Oceania), with a few events of special interest to Swedish learners (the Kalmar Union, the Stockholm Bloodbath, Poltava). Left out because the year is disputed or not a single year: the Battle of Tours (732 or 733), the Battle of Kadesh (the Egyptian chronologies differ), the founding of Rome (legendary), the East–West Schism (a process conventionally dated 1054), the Glorious Revolution (1688–1689), the Black Death (1346–1353), Gutenberg's first printing (uncertain), the Iranian Revolution (1978–1979). Left out because Wikidata's date conflicts with the commonly given one and the deck takes its dates from Wikidata: the Convention of Kanagawa (Wikidata 1864, commonly 1854) and the Suez Crisis (point in time 1957 alongside a 1956 start). Left out for lack of an independent check: the Battle of Talas (751). Further candidates were left out only to keep the deck near 80 cards and balanced between regions (among them Salamis, Zama, Trafalgar, Austerlitz, Lützen, the Wall Street Crash, the Salt March and the Bandung Conference)."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its only content source is Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0: the events' dates and the labels used on the cards come from it. The choice of events is the authoring agent's own and was not copied from any list. The card wording is the deck's own, apart from Wikidata labels. Store norske leksikon was used only to verify the years. Nothing from it is in the cards: no wording, no selection. Many of its articles are marked begrenset gjenbruk (restricted reuse), so it is recorded as all rights reserved. The provenance report quotes one or two short sentences per card as evidence, which SNL's terms allow even for restricted articles (“Du har fremdeles lov til å lenke til og sitere utdrag fra artikler merket med begrenset gjenbruk”). Facts such as the year of an event are not copyrightable. English Wikipedia's API was used only to look up four Wikidata item ids, and no Wikipedia text was used. A few Encyclopaedia Britannica, encyclopedia.com and infoplease URLs were requested (not recorded), but none returned content and nothing from them was used. The SNL sentences in the provenance report are short quotations made under SNL's quotation allowance; each is attributed by the article's URL, where the article's named authors and its licence (fri gjenbruk CC BY-SA 3.0 or begrenset gjenbruk) are shown. Treating every article under the stricter begrenset terms is also safe for the articles marked fri."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks against live Wikidata and a card-by-card cross-check of every year against Store norske leksikon"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All cards."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The builder's Wikidata checks cover every card: the property value that dates the card's event (P585, P580, P582, P571, P577, P619 or P1619), plus the English and Swedish Wikidata labels wherever a front uses one verbatim, and two Swedish aliases of related items. Every year was confirmed by a sentence in Store norske leksikon. The findings below record where the two sources' dates differ in day or framing (in no case in the year), how the card was framed to stay unambiguous, and the candidates that were dropped."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 87 cards: every year compared with the English Wikipedia lead of the card's Wikidata item, the builder's Wikidata checks re-run, notes and description read."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No wrong years. One warning (the Abbasid Caliphate did not strictly end in 1258) was fixed by rewording the front and adding a sourced backNote; the Hong Kong Island precision was fixed; two frontNote suggestions were already met by the cards; the year-only Wikidata checks are now listed in round 0."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Language and translation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 87 cards in English and Swedish, title, description, keywords, notes and language tags in the built TTL."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Both warnings fixed (Odovakar, the East India Company note), the Qin Shi Huangdi name, the Hijra and Bastille notes and the Nanjing subject were fixed; the Rwanda parity suggestion was declined. All other Swedish names of people and places were then checked against Wikidata and Swedish Wikipedia and are recorded in the method."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 87 cards' evidence (every SNL quote and Wikidata value re-fetched), sources, licence evidence, method, queries and licensing."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The licensing was sound. The documentation gaps were fixed: the unrecorded Britannica, encyclopedia.com and infoplease requests are now described honestly; the Xinhai backNote has evidence; every frontNote and backNote in the deck (not only the reviewer's examples) was compared with its evidence, so supporting sentences were added or the notes were cut back; the Teutoburg discrepancy and the stray ids are documented; and SNL attribution is stated."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "Final re-check (loop 1): changed cards, every third card, metadata, licensing and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: 42 cards (the 20 changed in round 3 plus every third card in dossier order), metadata, licensing and documentation."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors or warnings; five suggestions on documentation completeness and one Swedish wording. Each was verified against live Wikidata, Swedish Wikipedia and the dossier, and all five were applied."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing (fetched 2026-10-04): “All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.”"@en .

<https://snl.no/>
    dcterms:title "Store norske leksikon" ;
    dcterms:creator "Store norske leksikon and the named authors of each article" ;
    rdfs:comment "Each article carries its own licence. https://snl.no/.licenses/free (fetched 2026-10-04): “Fri gjenbruk betyr at artikkelen er lisensiert med Creative Commons -lisenen CC-BY-SA-3.0.” https://snl.no/.licenses/restricted (fetched 2026-10-04): “Hvis en artikkel i Store norske leksikon er merket med begrenset gjenbruk, kan du ikke gjenbruke, republisere eller bygge videre på artikkelen uten at du først har fått opphavspersonens tillatelse.” and “NB: Du har fremdeles lov til å lenke til og sitere utdrag fra artikler merket med begrenset gjenbruk uten å spørre opphavspersonen.” Most of the articles consulted are marked begrenset gjenbruk, so the source is recorded under the stricter terms and used for verification only."@en .

<https://sv.wikipedia.org/>
    dcterms:title "Swedish Wikipedia" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikipedia contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Upphovsr%C3%A4tt (fetched 2026-10-04): “De textbidrag du lämnar till Wikipedia publiceras under CC BY-SA 3.0 och GNU FDL.” Used for verification only, so nothing from it is in the cards."@en .

